  "live_records": {
    "q-327052": {
      "dom_id": "step-q327052",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251995/assets/22f309ffc9ff6ae4a43a5faae12e4ed952d65aec70f87a7e71f64594f0c61f92.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\frac{4}{3}a.$ So, we first draw the vector $\\frac{4}{3}a$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251995/assets/b8db5015e22e55282e180b1d81f7a2e1ca544dfe0f468dc4dedb6283bac2da5c.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $\\frac{4}{3}a.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{4}|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{4}b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}a+\\frac{1}{4}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\frac{1}{4}b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
