    "q-120250": {
      "dom_id": "step-q120250",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251995/assets/e1391a86176b1453c05f6ab43f2b5360fac00287e621d8917a142116990688b2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+3b$, find $\\lambda .$",
      "worked_solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251995/assets/eadc3cc79d5c0f9b58bd8afc6864dca8e5f794cf398921e2ad540b491819562f.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b+a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
