    "q-70140": {
      "dom_id": "step-q70140",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251995/assets/7578bb2e5b594161744d96d918fa75e0225e723138d010dba3ed635098e75ca2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=a+\\lambda b,$ find $\\lambda .$",
      "worked_solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251995/assets/4797ffa54b3c2e4d177a6c3d527b1aaf3f1460d3c509bca86395e5f360314d25.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=3|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=3b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+3b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
