    "q-120247": {
      "dom_id": "step-q120247",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251995/assets/2ea6acbf6574e970a798100729de9a92874bca90b8153db9883daa1cd6d98c2a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+2b$, find $\\lambda .$",
      "worked_solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251995/assets/3c31e9b0d926de6a3f8c9f35bd816349d6488c8b98864d8576fdbfc395bbd92d.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b+3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=3a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
