    "q-70144": {
      "dom_id": "step-q70144",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251995/assets/695c825905b268e40b7c77c681c608fcedfed4d0c5150f76224c4e60eb8ab045.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+2b$, find $\\lambda .$",
      "worked_solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251995/assets/7d388cdd1db5cfe59b447c3fc27b597fc4e42397966935592f56c1c9f4a598e6.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b+a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
