    "q-70148": {
      "dom_id": "step-q70148",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251995/assets/d3f77b108decdfe95fe06d1ace87ee7f844a4f937f8b67a9f14e750a09ae4d57.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a-b,$ find $\\lambda .$",
      "worked_solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251995/assets/7f29124776f69f370b3129313fd1c2fa850924f446c6f086dcacd170f1ca6d24.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2.5|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2.5a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-b-2.5a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-b=-2.5a-b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2.5\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
